262
00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:01.800
I mean, we're essentially in our work.

263
00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:04.530
Right, yeah, there must be thousands and thousands

264
00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:07.830
of hours of thousands archived classes, right?

265
00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:11.430
And so in addition to being head instructor,

266
00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:14.820
what is the role that you play as VP within the company?

267
00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:15.653
Like what does that look like?

268
00:12:15.653 --> 00:12:17.490
Yeah, well, my executive function

269
00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:18.900
is within the content department.

270
00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:21.540
So the chief content officer is my boss,

271
00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:26.540
and I am an advocate, I guess, for the talent,

272
00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:31.740
and as well as an intermediary, whether it's with marketing,

273
00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:34.830
whether it's with our engineering folks, our product team,

274
00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:39.660
if a fitness expertise is required,

275
00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:43.290
I'm usually the voice in that meeting in that room.

276
00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:44.280
And are you helpful

277
00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:46.620
with like finding the other experts out there

278
00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:50.610
to hire for these particular specific roles?

279
00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:52.830
I don't, as it relates to instructors?

280
00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:53.753
Yes.
Yeah.

281
00:12:53.753 --> 00:12:55.980
I mean, I'm usually one of the last interviews

282
00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:59.403
for blessing somebody to come onto the team.

283
00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:02.450
What's the question you ask these people?

284
00:13:03.870 --> 00:13:05.580
I'm kinda a hard (laughs).

285
00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:07.329
I know, like yeah.

286
00:13:07.329 --> 00:13:09.060
(indistinct) If the bus starts with you,

287
00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:10.500
if you're the last stop on the train,

288
00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:12.755
like what does that look like?

289
00:13:12.755 --> 00:13:16.870
This young person comes in with ideas of grander,

290
00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:20.220
like I'm gonna be a Peloton instructor

291
00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:22.380
and then you drop the gauntlet on them.

292
00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:25.380
It's not so much a gauntlet as it is listening

293
00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:28.620
to my gut instinct

294
00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:33.620
because there's always a little bit of artifice

295
00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:34.943
in an interview,

296
00:13:34.943 --> 00:13:37.200
'cause you're putting your best foot forward.

297
00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:42.200
But it can't be like I'm auditioning for a role in a movie.

298
00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:46.359
Because after thousands of hours of teaching a class,

299
00:13:46.359 --> 00:13:49.020
that's gonna erode, and you're gonna see

300
00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:50.790
what's the kernel really there.

301
00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:53.340
And there needs to be a point of view.

302
00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:54.420
Of course, there's an expertise,

303
00:13:54.420 --> 00:13:57.900
I mean, that's like, you wouldn't even audition

304
00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:01.017
unless you have a pedigree of athleticism and expertise.

305
00:14:01.017 --> 00:14:03.840
But you need that authentic charisma?

306
00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:04.673
You have to be able

307
00:14:04.673 --> 00:14:08.583
to host a television show unscripted while sprinting.

308
00:14:09.477 --> 00:14:12.450
(laughs) That's a very specific skillset.

309
00:14:12.450 --> 00:14:13.890
Yes.

310
00:14:13.890 --> 00:14:15.300
When I see you doing your thing,

311
00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:18.870
like it does look so natural, it is who you are,

312
00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:21.690
it's an extrapolation of your personality,

313
00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:24.870
but it's so high energy and me being much more

314
00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:27.060
of an introvert I think, there's gotta be days

315
00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:28.177
where she wakes up and she's like,

316
00:14:28.177 --> 00:14:30.360
"I really don't, I'm not up for it today."

317
00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:32.640
And you gotta go and do the thing, right?

318
00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:34.083
You gotta do the thing.

319
00:14:35.850 --> 00:14:39.360
I definitely have days, but I love it.

320
00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:44.360
I am so charged by what I get to do every day, it's insane.

321
00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:46.950
Like I sometimes I'm like,

322
00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:49.800
wait, so they're just giving me a microphone.

323
00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:52.350
Now that the scale is growing to what it is

324
00:14:52.350 --> 00:14:54.063
and it really is like a network.

325
00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:56.490
I'm like, wait, we're still doing this thing,

326
00:14:56.490 --> 00:14:57.570
like you're gonna make me up,

327
00:14:57.570 --> 00:15:00.060
and the red light goes on and we're just off.

328
00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:02.310
And that is really thrilling.

329
00:15:02.310 --> 00:15:06.060
How much prep do you put into what you're gonna say?

330
00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:08.280
The playlist, like all that kind of stuff

331
00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:09.750
that goes into the classes?

332
00:15:09.750 --> 00:15:11.820
The playlists and the class plan are locked.

333
00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:15.240
So I will know exactly what I'm doing,

334
00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:18.330
especially in a class that is more fitness-focused.

335
00:15:18.330 --> 00:15:20.340
If it's a HIIT class, if it's a Tabata class,

336
00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:23.910
I've been very prescriptive with that game plan.

337
00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:26.040
In terms of messaging and talking points,

338
00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:29.100
that's always, like 99% extemporaneous.

339
00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:30.360
I might have like bullets,

340
00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:32.340
like I wanna talk about mental focus,

341
00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:35.130
or I wanna talk about grit or I'll plug,

342
00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:36.390
or I'll mention like,

343
00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:40.697
remember this story about X training moment.

344
00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:44.250
But it won't be that, that's it,

345
00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:45.592
that's as detailed as it gets for me.

346
00:15:45.592 --> 00:15:47.220
So just get a little elevated heart rate

347
00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:49.307
and it just you're in the flow and it comes out.

348
00:15:49.307 --> 00:15:52.200
Flow, flow state, I mean the definition of flow state

349
00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:53.565
is what I'm teaching.

350
00:15:53.565 --> 00:15:55.140
Yeah, have you ever had a moment though

351
00:15:55.140 --> 00:15:56.553
where you just blank?

352
00:16:00.854 --> 00:16:01.687
No, not really.

353
00:16:01.687 --> 00:16:02.520
No.

354
00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:03.886
No, I'm so focused,

355
00:16:03.886 --> 00:16:08.730
I'm so hyper aware of everything that's going on.

356
00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:12.210
It's crazy how personal the relationship

357
00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:15.630
is between the participant and the instructor.

358
00:16:15.630 --> 00:16:17.790
Like I'm sure you've heard these stories a million times.

359
00:16:17.790 --> 00:16:19.590
Like, oh, you're my friend or you're the,

360
00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:24.210
like my deepest relationship is with my Peloton instructor.

361
00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:27.120
Everybody has their favorite, it's this weird,

362
00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:29.733
it is very much like a cultish thing.

363
00:16:30.852 --> 00:16:33.540
It is, it is.

364
00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:36.330
And now that the world is opening up again,

365
00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:39.180
Peloton instructors had lots of interactions

366
00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:41.490
with folks publicly pre-pandemic.

367
00:16:41.490 --> 00:16:44.250
But now that the world is opening up again,

368
00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:49.250
it's almost like people have this pent up emotion globally.

369
00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:51.720
And then when you see someone

370
00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:55.590
who you did have like intimate workout experiences with

371
00:16:55.590 --> 00:16:58.020
in your home when we were all collectively grieving

372
00:16:58.020 --> 00:16:59.523
and going through a lot,

373
00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:02.100
the outpouring of emotion

374
00:17:02.100 --> 00:17:05.850
that happens nearly on a daily basis is pretty intense.

375
00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:09.330
Like many (laughs) many tears.

376
00:17:09.330 --> 00:17:11.580
Yeah, how does that make you feel?

377
00:17:17.250 --> 00:17:19.383
There's a vulnerability that is like,

378
00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:23.730
kind of jarring in a way

379
00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:27.330
to be like in front of that kind of emotion

380
00:17:27.330 --> 00:17:28.710
is pretty intense,

381
00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:33.540
but also I totally get it because I have used the bike

382
00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:37.650
and used my teammate's classes truly to deal

383
00:17:37.650 --> 00:17:38.640
with a lot of heavy stuff.

384
00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:40.950
So I do, so I understand.

385
00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:43.500
Yeah, it's like, you're the face,

386
00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:45.930
but it represents something bigger,

387
00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:49.740
which is, that was a really hard year, two years.

388
00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:52.650
And this person helped me get through it

389
00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:56.670
by leading me through this routine that was so instrumental

390
00:17:56.670 --> 00:17:58.380
and kind of kept me grounded.

391
00:17:58.380 --> 00:17:59.700
And that is very emotional.

392
00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:01.980
So there's an intimacy to it, right?

393
00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:05.010
It's not like, it's different from a movie star,

394
00:18:05.010 --> 00:18:07.620
like, oh wow, I've seen that person on a huge screen

395
00:18:07.620 --> 00:18:09.780
and they're very good looking and charismatic

396
00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:13.470
versus like this person actually helped me.

397
00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:18.150
So I would suspect like it removes the creepy aspect of it.

398
00:18:18.150 --> 00:18:19.710
Maybe it's a little encroaching

399
00:18:19.710 --> 00:18:23.430
and boundary pushing a little bit, but at the same time,

400
00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:25.350
there's something so beautiful about it.

401
00:18:25.350 --> 00:18:26.910
It's largely beautiful.

402
00:18:26.910 --> 00:18:29.910
I mean, sometimes there's a little bit of like,

403
00:18:29.910 --> 00:18:33.060
boundaryless action for sure.

404
00:18:33.060 --> 00:18:37.680
But for the most part it's really stunning to witness.

405
00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:39.810
Yeah, well, you're good with boundaries though,

406
00:18:39.810 --> 00:18:41.675
you're the queen of no.
I'm very good at--

407
00:18:41.675 --> 00:18:43.290
But let's talk about no a little bit.

408
00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:45.236
Like I'm still working my nos.

409
00:18:45.236 --> 00:18:49.020
(laughs) I love no, no is my favorite word.

410
00:18:49.020 --> 00:18:53.280
Yeah, I use my no to protect my yes.

411
00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:56.340
I say no to most things

412
00:18:56.340 --> 00:19:01.340
and I am pretty famous for slashing.

413
00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:05.250
Like somebody tries to put a 90 minute calendar appointment

414
00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:06.780
on my calendar next week and I was like,

415
00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:11.160
that's literally not gonna happen ever,

416
00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:13.323
like I'm maybe giving you 25 minutes,

417
00:19:15.848 --> 00:19:19.320
and I really own my no to protect the yes.

418
00:19:19.320 --> 00:19:21.690
How do you know when it's a yes?

419
00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:22.523
What's the rule?

420
00:19:22.523 --> 00:19:24.480
It's a hell yes, or it's a no, thanks.

421
00:19:24.480 --> 00:19:26.040
If it doesn't feel like a hell yes,

422
00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:27.903
especially since becoming a mother,

423
00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:33.930
if something doesn't feel like it's lighting me up,

424
00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:35.220
then it's a no.

425
00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:37.740
And of course there's the stuff like you pay your taxes,

426
00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:41.670
you do, there's stuff that doesn't feel great to do,

427
00:19:41.670 --> 00:19:42.870
but it's moving the needle.

428
00:19:42.870 --> 00:19:45.240
You're talking about like opportunities that come your way

429
00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:47.370
and the bigger that you get,

430
00:19:47.370 --> 00:19:49.410
the cooler the opportunities are

431
00:19:49.410 --> 00:19:50.970
and the more tempting they are,

432
00:19:50.970 --> 00:19:55.200
and that hell yes it can be there.

433
00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:58.530
It can be a hell yes but also like this is a distraction

434
00:19:58.530 --> 00:19:59.910
from what I'm actually trying to do,

435
00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:01.920
or it's gonna take me away from being a mother

436
00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:02.850
or those other things.

437
00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:04.885
That's when it gets really hard and confusing.

438
00:20:04.885 --> 00:20:09.180
Like, "Hey, do you want a first class ticket to go to Dubai

439
00:20:09.180 --> 00:20:10.380
and do this cool?"

440
00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:11.670
You're like, "Yeah, I'd like to do that,"

441
00:20:11.670 --> 00:20:13.027
but then you're like,

442
00:20:13.027 --> 00:20:15.630
"Yeah, that's really gonna screw my stuff up,

443
00:20:15.630 --> 00:20:17.091
like I probably shouldn't do that."

444
00:20:17.091 --> 00:20:17.924
Agreed.

445
00:20:17.924 --> 00:20:21.780
It's hard, these are problems of abundance.

446
00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:24.060
These are problem problems of privilege of course,

447
00:20:24.060 --> 00:20:25.140
but I've experienced it

448
00:20:25.140 --> 00:20:30.140
and I'm not as great with the no, it's hard,

449
00:20:30.660 --> 00:20:32.430
'cause I wanna do all the cool stuff.

450
00:20:32.430 --> 00:20:34.020
Yeah, but you gotta access.

451
00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:37.890
Then I spread myself thin and then I like burn out

452
00:20:37.890 --> 00:20:39.630
and I have all these other problems.

453
00:20:39.630 --> 00:20:41.424
Oh my God, but that's so not worth it.

454
00:20:41.424 --> 00:20:44.940
I think about the future version,

455
00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:48.000
like I think about the version of myself

456
00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:50.970
at the end of that journey,

457
00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:53.913
as much as I, to the extent that I can contemplate things

458
00:20:53.913 --> 00:20:55.590
that I haven't experienced yet.

459
00:20:55.590 --> 00:21:00.540
And I think about the burnout version of myself

460
00:21:00.540 --> 00:21:05.280
that I have so much grace and compassion

461
00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:06.663
for that version of myself,

462
00:21:08.610 --> 00:21:11.360
largely when I was doing like the 80 hour lawyer weeks,

463
00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:18.380
any element of that is like threatens who I,

464
00:21:21.503 --> 00:21:24.330
like it just shakes me to my core to even go back there.

465
00:21:24.330 --> 00:21:25.163
And I love--

466
00:21:25.163 --> 00:21:27.300
Meaning like when you were doing the 80 hour weeks

467
00:21:27.300 --> 00:21:29.550
and not having agency over your time?

468
00:21:29.550 --> 00:21:32.340
Yeah, there was a powerlessness

469
00:21:32.340 --> 00:21:37.340
as well as just true fatigue, you know?

470
00:21:37.770 --> 00:21:41.070
And I do love hustling.

471
00:21:41.070 --> 00:21:42.960
I feel like the pendulum has swung that now,

472
00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:45.693
like hustle is a dirty word and people are like,

473
00:21:45.693 --> 00:21:48.030
and I'm like, where's your work ethic?

474
00:21:48.030 --> 00:21:49.860
Like give me a freaking break.

475
00:21:49.860 --> 00:21:51.090
So you just have to be in,

476
00:21:51.090 --> 00:21:55.530
I define hustle as grit and work ethic,

477
00:21:55.530 --> 00:21:59.820
meeting your definition of success and purpose.

478
00:21:59.820 --> 00:22:04.503
And I think those two combined create a beautiful momentum,

479
00:22:06.463 --> 00:22:08.820
but I don't believe in redlining obviously,

480
00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:12.330
and that no pain, no gain mentality is harmful,

481
00:22:12.330 --> 00:22:14.250
but we do have to have an element of grit

482
00:22:14.250 --> 00:22:15.240
and work ethic still.

483
00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:16.973
A hundred percent.
So it's finding the balance.

484
00:22:16.973 --> 00:22:19.380
I think that pendulum has swung a little bit too far

485
00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:21.990
where it's like, don't hustle,

486
00:22:21.990 --> 00:22:23.340
make sure that you're resting.

487
00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:25.320
It's like, look sleep's important, recovery,

488
00:22:25.320 --> 00:22:26.940
all these things are important,

489
00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:31.590
but a lot of the people that are over-indexing on that

490
00:22:31.590 --> 00:22:33.750
don't have any of the hustle to begin with,

491
00:22:33.750 --> 00:22:35.430
and could use a little bit of that.

492
00:22:35.430 --> 00:22:39.557
Yeah, the listlessness, the like...

493
00:22:40.650 --> 00:22:41.850
It's sort of rubber stamping

494
00:22:41.850 --> 00:22:45.870
or permitting you to kind of be the lazy person

495
00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:50.070
that you have been historically, it's misused.

496
00:22:50.070 --> 00:22:53.130
It like makes me break out into hives.

497
00:22:53.130 --> 00:22:54.515
Like I actually cannot.

498
00:22:54.515 --> 00:22:56.682
I can't, I can't, I can't.

499
00:22:57.887 --> 00:23:00.630
So what is the, I know you hate this word balance,

500
00:23:00.630 --> 00:23:03.840
but like what is the fulcrum point on that for you?

501
00:23:03.840 --> 00:23:06.030
Like it's changed, I wanna talk about you being a mother,

502
00:23:06.030 --> 00:23:10.530
but obviously being a mother to an infant changes

503
00:23:10.530 --> 00:23:12.137
that equation quite a bit.

504
00:23:12.137 --> 00:23:16.470
I don't believe in balance., I don't believe that,

505
00:23:16.470 --> 00:23:19.140
okay, I think balance is a harmful concept

506
00:23:19.140 --> 00:23:22.920
when we perceive it to be an even Stephen

507
00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:25.590
that like everything gets the same percentage

508
00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:28.170
of your 24 hour day or whatever that a hundred percent

509
00:23:28.170 --> 00:23:30.063
of you is in the world.

510
00:23:31.170 --> 00:23:32.310
There isn't gonna be balance.

511
00:23:32.310 --> 00:23:33.450
I mean, there are some days

512
00:23:33.450 --> 00:23:37.560
where I'm feeling like I'm giving 20% to my marriage

513
00:23:37.560 --> 00:23:38.940
and my husband's giving the 80%.

514
00:23:38.940 --> 00:23:40.980
And then there are some days where it feels more 50/50,

515
00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:42.840
and then there are some days,

516
00:23:42.840 --> 00:23:46.110
you have to show up in alignment with important to you.

517
00:23:46.110 --> 00:23:50.970
And I guess that is a more palatable definition

518
00:23:50.970 --> 00:23:55.410
of balance to me is what is my short list of values

519
00:23:55.410 --> 00:23:56.730
and what's important.

520
00:23:56.730 --> 00:23:59.070
And so that goes back to like the hell yes

521
00:23:59.070 --> 00:24:00.210
or the no thank you.

522
00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:03.420
Is that if a business opportunity is coming my way

523
00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:06.120
or a philanthropic thing that's pulling out my heartstrings.

524
00:24:06.120 --> 00:24:09.330
Like if it's really not moving the needle on the short list

525
00:24:09.330 --> 00:24:10.890
of things that's important to me,

526
00:24:10.890 --> 00:24:13.733
and the top of that is certainly my family and my daughter,

527
00:24:14.640 --> 00:24:16.980
protecting that time.

528
00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:19.740
The stuff that I am devoted to professionally

529
00:24:19.740 --> 00:24:23.400
has gotta light me up and make me feel like a superhero.

530
00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:26.250
Yeah, the balance thing is tough

531
00:24:26.250 --> 00:24:31.250
because I think it ends up shaming people

532
00:24:31.290 --> 00:24:34.080
because everybody's walking around measuring themselves

533
00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:36.870
against some idealized notion

534
00:24:36.870 --> 00:24:40.440
of how everything should fit on on the daily.

535
00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:42.360
I just don't believe anybody,

536
00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:47.360
particularly people who excel in their lives are living up

537
00:24:48.150 --> 00:24:49.320
to that themselves.

538
00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:52.860
So I like to think of it as being completely present

539
00:24:52.860 --> 00:24:54.900
for whatever it is that you're doing,

540
00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:58.050
and giving it your all, and then toggling out of it

541
00:24:58.050 --> 00:25:00.240
and making sure that all of those buckets

542
00:25:00.240 --> 00:25:02.820
over an extended period of time are being filled,

543
00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:07.380
but understanding and being okay with them not being

544
00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:10.170
on an equal level of waterline

545
00:25:10.170 --> 00:25:12.360
on a daily basis or even a weekly basis,

546
00:25:12.360 --> 00:25:15.390
or even a monthly basis sometimes depending upon

547
00:25:15.390 --> 00:25:17.430
how immersive a certain project

548
00:25:17.430 --> 00:25:21.363
or kind of thing that you're pushing forward involves.

549
00:25:22.325 --> 00:25:26.160
I don't think people understand energy to be currency.

550
00:25:26.160 --> 00:25:30.510
Like if we had currency meters

551
00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:33.330
of our energetic exchanges

552
00:25:33.330 --> 00:25:37.177
whether it's the person in your family that you're like,

553
00:25:37.177 --> 00:25:38.640
"Oh, gosh, so and so is really

554
00:25:38.640 --> 00:25:40.290
just like an energy vampire,"

555
00:25:40.290 --> 00:25:42.900
think what is that expenditure costing you?

556
00:25:42.900 --> 00:25:47.070
And what value are you putting on your own vibrancy

557
00:25:47.070 --> 00:25:51.570
and buoyancy and how are you centering experiences

558
00:25:51.570 --> 00:25:56.570
that reward and really, really allow you to increase

559
00:26:00.630 --> 00:26:04.050
that energetic currency, that bank account.

560
00:26:04.050 --> 00:26:05.943
Like there should be an app with a thing on your,

561
00:26:05.943 --> 00:26:08.250
like that measures that

562
00:26:08.250 --> 00:26:10.530
and then there's like graphs it's like,

563
00:26:10.530 --> 00:26:14.010
you devoted this much energy to your in-laws, this, this,

564
00:26:14.010 --> 00:26:16.846
you devoted this much energy to Netflix

565
00:26:16.846 --> 00:26:19.740
and color code them based upon your values.

566
00:26:19.740 --> 00:26:22.170
Like, so red would be bad (laughs).

567
00:26:22.170 --> 00:26:23.903
I wish.
And then you could be like,

568
00:26:23.903 --> 00:26:26.936
"Wow, what am I doing with how I'm expending my energy?"

569
00:26:26.936 --> 00:26:29.760
But we have that, right?

570
00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:32.610
When we listen to our intuition and when we listen to,

571
00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:36.210
like, we have that innately, but we're very distracted

572
00:26:36.210 --> 00:26:39.210
and we sometimes we don't like the answer to that intuition.

573
00:26:41.760 --> 00:26:44.010
Yeah, it's hard, it's hard.

574
00:26:44.010 --> 00:26:47.760
You have to really be calibrated internally

575
00:26:47.760 --> 00:26:50.357
to have that level of self-awareness.

576
00:26:50.357 --> 00:26:52.350
'Cause I think we all just kind of do it

577
00:26:52.350 --> 00:26:54.450
and we don't concentrate enough

578
00:26:54.450 --> 00:26:57.213
on what that actually looks like.

579
00:26:58.170 --> 00:27:00.960
So yeah, that's a cool idea.

580
00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:02.220
Well, let's talk about being a mom.

581
00:27:02.220 --> 00:27:03.330
I mean, this is the big thing

582
00:27:03.330 --> 00:27:07.074
that's happened to you over the last year, congratulations.

583
00:27:07.074 --> 00:27:07.907
Thank you.

584
00:27:07.907 --> 00:27:10.680
I've yet to meet her, Drew dropped you off, right?

585
00:27:10.680 --> 00:27:11.850
He's coming back?

