and I just looked it up on the internet, and I, info at,

1289
00:59:39.870 --> 00:59:42.097
there were like, 20 employees at the time, and I was like,

1290
00:59:42.097 --> 00:59:44.850
"Let's do this, I am your chic."

1291
00:59:44.850 --> 00:59:46.050
And they said yes (laughs).

1292
00:59:46.050 --> 00:59:47.550
Wow!

1293
00:59:47.550 --> 00:59:50.197
So when people come to you and say,

1294
00:59:50.197 --> 00:59:54.120
"I'm looking at trying to make some version

1295
00:59:54.120 --> 00:59:56.490
of your life pivot journaling,"

1296
00:59:56.490 --> 00:59:57.840
what else do you tell them?

1297
01:00:01.440 --> 01:00:04.673
I think the three most relevant questions

1298
01:00:06.724 --> 01:00:11.724
that I asked myself during that transition period,

1299
01:00:12.360 --> 01:00:16.110
and still continue to ask myself, "Why not me?"

1300
01:00:16.110 --> 01:00:20.430
You know, like, we tend to other people's greatness

1301
01:00:20.430 --> 01:00:22.980
instead of understanding that in the kaleidoscope

1302
01:00:22.980 --> 01:00:24.240
of how we see things,

1303
01:00:24.240 --> 01:00:27.390
what if that greatness is being refracted as potential?

1304
01:00:27.390 --> 01:00:32.340
Like, now I see somebody else's greatness as a potential

1305
01:00:32.340 --> 01:00:36.300
of my own, not in a apples to apples comparison of course,

1306
01:00:36.300 --> 01:00:38.940
necessarily, but as potential.

1307
01:00:38.940 --> 01:00:43.940
So folks, ask yourself, "Why not me?"

1308
01:00:46.410 --> 01:00:50.073
And the second question that I've asked myself,

1309
01:00:50.910 --> 01:00:54.840
which we talked about a little was like, "What is my why?"

1310
01:00:54.840 --> 01:00:57.150
I do believe like, identifying that

1311
01:00:57.150 --> 01:00:59.490
with as much specificity as possible,

1312
01:00:59.490 --> 01:01:01.740
and acknowledging that sometimes that's gonna change

1313
01:01:01.740 --> 01:01:04.230
in different seasons is freeing.

1314
01:01:04.230 --> 01:01:06.360
I give myself permission to change my mind

1315
01:01:06.360 --> 01:01:08.790
as a stubborn individual, as a stubborn Virgo,

1316
01:01:08.790 --> 01:01:11.340
former lawyer that is incredibly freeing,

1317
01:01:11.340 --> 01:01:13.680
to give myself permission to change my mind.

1318
01:01:13.680 --> 01:01:18.680
And the third most exciting question

1319
01:01:18.810 --> 01:01:20.760
is what decision would I make if I were twice

1320
01:01:20.760 --> 01:01:23.010
as confident and twice as strong?

1321
01:01:23.010 --> 01:01:23.970
And that--

1322
01:01:23.970 --> 01:01:24.803
That's a good one.

1323
01:01:24.803 --> 01:01:27.000
That one, I asked myself a lot, especially postpartum.

1324
01:01:27.000 --> 01:01:28.440
It was like, "What decision would I make

1325
01:01:28.440 --> 01:01:29.850
if I were twice as confident?"

1326
01:01:29.850 --> 01:01:32.220
And that actually is a nice tool

1327
01:01:32.220 --> 01:01:33.870
against imposter syndrome too.

1328
01:01:33.870 --> 01:01:37.140
It's kind of an alter ego exercise.

1329
01:01:37.140 --> 01:01:42.140
Yes, and I love like, the lore around superheroes

1330
01:01:42.600 --> 01:01:43.860
and stuff like that.

1331
01:01:43.860 --> 01:01:48.270
And actually, this is related to Web 3

1332
01:01:48.270 --> 01:01:51.600
is like, that level of enchantment I think

1333
01:01:51.600 --> 01:01:56.600
is really exciting of like, I do talk a lot about royalty,

1334
01:01:57.840 --> 01:02:01.080
and polishing crowns, and I think there's a level

1335
01:02:01.080 --> 01:02:04.620
of self-determination and imbuing respect

1336
01:02:04.620 --> 01:02:06.210
when you call someone a king or a queen,

1337
01:02:06.210 --> 01:02:07.890
or non-binary royalty.

1338
01:02:07.890 --> 01:02:12.720
And there is like, another version of myself that I try

1339
01:02:12.720 --> 01:02:17.300
to honor, whether you call it an avatar, alter ego.

1340
01:02:20.250 --> 01:02:21.330
When you conjure that,

1341
01:02:21.330 --> 01:02:23.250
what does that look like in your mind?

1342
01:02:23.250 --> 01:02:25.140
Like, she is regal.

1343
01:02:25.140 --> 01:02:28.830
She has earned jewels in her crown through grit.

1344
01:02:28.830 --> 01:02:33.830
And I, yeah, I mean, it's like a version of myself

1345
01:02:35.880 --> 01:02:38.850
that is otherworldly, but like, she's on a throne, honey,

1346
01:02:38.850 --> 01:02:40.860
and don't play with her.

1347
01:02:40.860 --> 01:02:44.130
And I really think about that version of myself,

1348
01:02:44.130 --> 01:02:47.880
or that imagined version of myself often.

1349
01:02:47.880 --> 01:02:52.770
And I'm creating a kingdom with my husband.

1350
01:02:52.770 --> 01:02:56.733
And Athena is gonna be a part of that,

1351
01:02:59.310 --> 01:03:00.870
but I also wanted her to like earn--

1352
01:03:00.870 --> 01:03:03.960
Appropriately named in the goddess vernacular.

1353
01:03:03.960 --> 01:03:07.800
Yeah, but I also want her to be able to earn

1354
01:03:07.800 --> 01:03:10.680
and define her own way, you know?

1355
01:03:10.680 --> 01:03:13.140
So that's an interesting thing that I'll navigate

1356
01:03:13.140 --> 01:03:15.723
as she gets older, but yeah.

1357
01:03:17.160 --> 01:03:19.680
Another big piece of that you talk about

1358
01:03:19.680 --> 01:03:21.153
is being unapologetic,

1359
01:03:22.110 --> 01:03:24.600
but that is befitting of a queen, right?

1360
01:03:24.600 --> 01:03:26.010
Or of royalty.

1361
01:03:26.010 --> 01:03:27.000
Yeah,

1362
01:03:27.000 --> 01:03:32.000
Well, I've learned to use my voice

1363
01:03:35.490 --> 01:03:38.790
even when it's shaky, like, use it

1364
01:03:38.790 --> 01:03:40.863
and you'll find your people.

1365
01:03:42.810 --> 01:03:46.140
And that paired with having enough confidence

1366
01:03:46.140 --> 01:03:50.490
to know when you should have done better.

1367
01:03:50.490 --> 01:03:55.190
You know, I think using your voice, even when it shakes,

1368
01:03:58.470 --> 01:04:01.680
and having an ability to admit when you're wrong

1369
01:04:01.680 --> 01:04:05.133
and change your mind are lethal combinations.

1370
01:04:06.720 --> 01:04:09.180
And then not fearing other people's success,

1371
01:04:09.180 --> 01:04:11.250
which we talked about earlier.

1372
01:04:11.250 --> 01:04:13.680
Yeah, I find it totally intoxicating

1373
01:04:13.680 --> 01:04:17.490
and palpable when somebody is like, "Step into your power,"

1374
01:04:17.490 --> 01:04:20.220
like, "Polish your crown baby."

1375
01:04:20.220 --> 01:04:23.913
Like, that just means that I am surrounded by royalty.

1376
01:04:24.750 --> 01:04:25.683
That excites me.

1377
01:04:27.090 --> 01:04:29.700
Let's talk about the T1,

1378
01:04:29.700 --> 01:04:31.800
that's another big piece in your story

1379
01:04:31.800 --> 01:04:33.303
being type one diabetic.

1380
01:04:34.830 --> 01:04:36.630
You're healthy, everything's good, right?

1381
01:04:36.630 --> 01:04:38.670
Thank goodness.
Your health and stuff,

1382
01:04:38.670 --> 01:04:40.556
with regards to that.

1383
01:04:40.556 --> 01:04:43.556
I've been playing around with a CGM.

1384
01:04:45.589 --> 01:04:46.422
Oh, cool.

1385
01:04:46.422 --> 01:04:49.590
All through levels, and it's been really interesting.

1386
01:04:49.590 --> 01:04:51.870
And I've thought of you often as I'm like,

1387
01:04:51.870 --> 01:04:55.380
checking my blank glucose, 'cause we go to dinner

1388
01:04:55.380 --> 01:04:56.557
and you're kind of, I'm like,

1389
01:04:56.557 --> 01:04:57.690
"Why is she so distracted?"

1390
01:04:57.690 --> 01:04:59.430
And it's like, no, as you're doing right now.

1391
01:04:59.430 --> 01:05:01.320
I am doing right now, yeah (laughs).

1392
01:05:01.320 --> 01:05:03.170
I like how it's on the Apple Watch.

1393
01:05:04.470 --> 01:05:06.840
But it's been really interesting to see that relationship

1394
01:05:06.840 --> 01:05:08.460
between the foods that I'm eating

1395
01:05:08.460 --> 01:05:13.460
and how it's impacting that graph of blood glucose going up,

1396
01:05:15.630 --> 01:05:18.030
going down, when is it plateau?

1397
01:05:18.030 --> 01:05:19.230
What really spikes it?

1398
01:05:19.230 --> 01:05:20.063
What doesn't?

1399
01:05:20.063 --> 01:05:22.560
How does sleep, rest, recovery, training,

1400
01:05:22.560 --> 01:05:25.560
all these other lifestyle habits impact that?

1401
01:05:25.560 --> 01:05:27.540
That's probably been the most revelatory, it's like,

1402
01:05:27.540 --> 01:05:30.600
obviously, if you eat something that's high

1403
01:05:30.600 --> 01:05:33.300
in simple carbohydrates, it's gonna do this.

1404
01:05:33.300 --> 01:05:35.280
I've also noticed that foods that are higher

1405
01:05:35.280 --> 01:05:36.963
in fat will do the same thing.

1406
01:05:38.790 --> 01:05:41.370
I think the danger with this is that people

1407
01:05:41.370 --> 01:05:44.790
who aren't steeped in the science can make very binary,

1408
01:05:44.790 --> 01:05:47.790
reductive conclusions from that data.

1409
01:05:47.790 --> 01:05:50.370
I think the truth is probably much more complex,

1410
01:05:50.370 --> 01:05:55.020
but as somebody who's been dealing with this condition

1411
01:05:55.020 --> 01:05:57.720
that you have for a very long time and is very schooled

1412
01:05:57.720 --> 01:06:01.401
on how these CGMs work and all of that,

1413
01:06:01.401 --> 01:06:02.940
like, what have you learned about yourself,

1414
01:06:02.940 --> 01:06:05.820
and how do you think this kind of technology

1415
01:06:05.820 --> 01:06:08.700
could be helpful for somebody who doesn't suffer

1416
01:06:08.700 --> 01:06:09.870
from type one?

1417
01:06:09.870 --> 01:06:11.220
Oh, that's interesting.

1418
01:06:11.220 --> 01:06:12.990
I mean, yeah, body awareness is key.

1419
01:06:12.990 --> 01:06:15.960
I'm very grateful that I was already plant based,

1420
01:06:15.960 --> 01:06:18.510
and I was already an athlete when I was diagnosed.

1421
01:06:18.510 --> 01:06:20.850
So there's a level of self awareness

1422
01:06:20.850 --> 01:06:25.620
that I already had going into my type one diagnosis.

1423
01:06:25.620 --> 01:06:27.577
And then I just told myself,

1424
01:06:27.577 --> 01:06:30.780
"I'm gonna continue to run these finish lines.

1425
01:06:30.780 --> 01:06:31.860
I'ma cross these finish lines.

1426
01:06:31.860 --> 01:06:33.030
I'm gonna continue to lift weights.

1427
01:06:33.030 --> 01:06:34.500
I'm gonna continue to teach a Peloton."

1428
01:06:34.500 --> 01:06:35.700
Like, actually, I was just on the eve

1429
01:06:35.700 --> 01:06:37.560
of teaching a Peloton when I was diagnosed.

1430
01:06:37.560 --> 01:06:39.510
And this, the time is different now,

1431
01:06:39.510 --> 01:06:42.420
because now there's an acceptance of this.

1432
01:06:42.420 --> 01:06:44.040
But when you were starting,

1433
01:06:44.040 --> 01:06:47.280
I know that people thought that that was inadvisable

1434
01:06:47.280 --> 01:06:48.990
or perhaps even very dangerous.

1435
01:06:48.990 --> 01:06:53.160
Oh, I was told, "You're not gonna run ultras anymore."

1436
01:06:53.160 --> 01:06:55.710
Like, "How are you gonna be able to teach

1437
01:06:55.710 --> 01:06:59.610
in front of a camera and not having to go..."

1438
01:06:59.610 --> 01:07:02.010
The doctors couldn't even contemplate me teaching

1439
01:07:02.010 --> 01:07:02.843
in front of millions of people

1440
01:07:02.843 --> 01:07:07.593
and not having to pause the class to leave and do whatever,

1441
01:07:08.610 --> 01:07:10.560
which knock on wood has never happened.

1442
01:07:14.070 --> 01:07:15.720
I mean, the technology helps a lot,

1443
01:07:15.720 --> 01:07:17.640
so I have a Dexcom G6,

1444
01:07:17.640 --> 01:07:22.433
and I loop that basically, you create like a bridge

1445
01:07:23.610 --> 01:07:26.790
through an app and through this Bluetooth device

1446
01:07:26.790 --> 01:07:31.790
that links the blood sugar device

1447
01:07:32.730 --> 01:07:35.460
with an insulin distribution system.

1448
01:07:35.460 --> 01:07:38.760
Right, and you can just refresh it every couple minutes

1449
01:07:38.760 --> 01:07:40.830
or whatever, and you can kind of see pretty much

1450
01:07:40.830 --> 01:07:42.690
in real time what's happening.

1451
01:07:42.690 --> 01:07:44.790
You're seeing the numbers in real time,

1452
01:07:44.790 --> 01:07:49.200
but I'm also administering like, the device, the algorithm,

1453
01:07:52.320 --> 01:07:55.470
is administering the insulin based on the setting.

1454
01:07:55.470 --> 01:07:58.050
So it's reading it and deciding how much, if,

1455
01:07:58.050 --> 01:08:01.800
and when the insulin gets right from the device on your arm.

1456
01:08:01.800 --> 01:08:04.470
Right, yeah, and that helps hugely.

1457
01:08:04.470 --> 01:08:08.023
And I approached my care, I was like,

1458
01:08:09.900 --> 01:08:11.850
I can either resist and get frustrated,

1459
01:08:11.850 --> 01:08:13.530
or I can get curious.

1460
01:08:13.530 --> 01:08:17.730
And I just got really freaking curious about everything,

1461
01:08:17.730 --> 01:08:20.830
from like, if I work out at this time and I eat this

1462
01:08:22.050 --> 01:08:24.330
portion of my smoothie before this type of workout,

1463
01:08:24.330 --> 01:08:27.840
and I, now I kind of (laughs)

1464
01:08:27.840 --> 01:08:31.020
I know how to move the needle,

1465
01:08:31.020 --> 01:08:34.860
you know, all those little nuances matter so much.

1466
01:08:34.860 --> 01:08:37.170
Yeah, and they're gonna be very specific

1467
01:08:37.170 --> 01:08:39.780
to you as an individual, and also very differentiated

1468
01:08:39.780 --> 01:08:43.320
because you have type one, what's interesting now

1469
01:08:43.320 --> 01:08:48.320
is that there's adoption of a version of this device.

1470
01:08:48.720 --> 01:08:50.580
Obviously, it's not injecting you with insulin,

1471
01:08:50.580 --> 01:08:53.370
but with mainstream people.

1472
01:08:53.370 --> 01:08:56.070
And I think there's a portion

1473
01:08:56.070 --> 01:09:00.210
of society that is frowning upon this.

1474
01:09:00.210 --> 01:09:02.190
Like, this thing is intended

1475
01:09:02.190 --> 01:09:03.600
for people with diabetes, right?

1476
01:09:03.600 --> 01:09:06.660
Like, this shouldn't be being used by mainstream people.

1477
01:09:06.660 --> 01:09:07.987
And then the counterpoint being like,

1478
01:09:07.987 --> 01:09:09.630
"Hey, all information is good.

1479
01:09:09.630 --> 01:09:11.340
This is gonna help us make better decisions.

1480
01:09:11.340 --> 01:09:12.900
We're gonna get these massive data sets.

1481
01:09:12.900 --> 01:09:15.120
We're gonna better understand how foods

1482
01:09:15.120 --> 01:09:18.600
and lifestyle habits are impacting blood glucose,"

1483
01:09:18.600 --> 01:09:21.360
which is really important in helping people prevent

1484
01:09:21.360 --> 01:09:23.310
themselves from getting type two

1485
01:09:23.310 --> 01:09:25.440
or other lifestyle illnesses.

1486
01:09:25.440 --> 01:09:26.910
For sure, it's a massive biomarker.

1487
01:09:26.910 --> 01:09:31.910
I mean, I don't, unless the, the former criticism

1488
01:09:33.240 --> 01:09:38.240
is presuming that having that type

1489
01:09:40.350 --> 01:09:45.350
of technology widely available somehow inhibits

1490
01:09:45.480 --> 01:09:48.990
or prevents type ones from having access to it,

1491
01:09:48.990 --> 01:09:50.010
I don't see the issue.

1492
01:09:50.010 --> 01:09:50.940
It's like--

1493
01:09:50.940 --> 01:09:52.650
Right, unless there's a supply chain problem.

1494
01:09:52.650 --> 01:09:53.483
You're right, unless--

1495
01:09:53.483 --> 01:09:54.720
I think part of the criticism also

1496
01:09:54.720 --> 01:09:56.610
is what I referenced earlier that people

1497
01:09:56.610 --> 01:09:59.580
who don't really understand will make

1498
01:09:59.580 --> 01:10:04.580
really reductive conclusions about sort of A plus B equal C

1499
01:10:05.970 --> 01:10:08.790
when that's probably not really the case.

1500
01:10:08.790 --> 01:10:11.400
And then sort of saying, "Well, this is the case,

1501
01:10:11.400 --> 01:10:12.990
so here's how I need to eat,"

1502
01:10:12.990 --> 01:10:15.240
or, "Here's this is happening because of this,"

1503
01:10:15.240 --> 01:10:18.580
where it's probably a much more complex

1504
01:10:19.950 --> 01:10:23.640
physiological mechanism than what is revealed

1505
01:10:23.640 --> 01:10:25.230
by a simple graph.

1506
01:10:25.230 --> 01:10:27.240
Sure, but you could say that about anything.

1507
01:10:27.240 --> 01:10:28.433
You could say that about the sleep trackers, you could say,

1508
01:10:28.433 --> 01:10:31.770
I mean, literally any of this biometric data,

1509
01:10:31.770 --> 01:10:35.520
you could be making reductive decisions that aren't nuanced.

1510
01:10:35.520 --> 01:10:39.423
But I think that we can use this data.

1511
01:10:42.090 --> 01:10:44.160
I would love for people to be more intentional

1512
01:10:44.160 --> 01:10:45.330
about their food choices

1513
01:10:45.330 --> 01:10:47.370
and see how their bodies are reacting.

1514
01:10:47.370 --> 01:10:49.620
Most people don't understand that you shouldn't feel like

1515
01:10:49.620 --> 01:10:52.020
you were hit by a truck and you're so lethargic

1516
01:10:52.020 --> 01:10:54.150
and fatigued every day at three o'clock

1517
01:10:54.150 --> 01:10:55.290
after you've had lunch, you know?

1518
01:10:55.290 --> 01:10:58.680
And it's like, if that data point helps people understand

1519
01:10:58.680 --> 01:10:59.675
that, then great.

1520
01:10:59.675 --> 01:11:01.220
Yeah, and being able to see like, "Oh man,

1521
01:11:01.220 --> 01:11:04.440
like, I feel that way, 'cause yeah, here's this."

1522
01:11:04.440 --> 01:11:05.910
And like, "Oh, it's taking me a long time

1523
01:11:05.910 --> 01:11:07.530
to get back up here.

1524
01:11:07.530 --> 01:11:10.260
The other day I ate this and I bounce back more quickly."

1525
01:11:10.260 --> 01:11:12.840
Like, all of these are really helpful, I think.

1526
01:11:12.840 --> 01:11:16.230
It's potentially transformative technology

1527
01:11:16.230 --> 01:11:18.150
when it's sort of adopted at scale,

1528
01:11:18.150 --> 01:11:20.250
which I think will happen at some point.

1529
01:11:20.250 --> 01:11:23.100
Definitely, especially if people can, and I mean,

1530
01:11:23.100 --> 01:11:25.530
listen, diabetes, especially type two is an epidemic.

1531
01:11:25.530 --> 01:11:27.951
If we can prevent folks from reaching that point of crisis--

1532
01:11:27.951 --> 01:11:31.650
The percent of Americans that are gonna be diabetic

1533
01:11:31.650 --> 01:11:33.674
or prediabetic by like, 2050, it's insane.

1534
01:11:33.674 --> 01:11:35.700
Yeah, it's pretty terrifying.

1535
01:11:35.700 --> 01:11:37.380
So we need to do something,

1536
01:11:37.380 --> 01:11:40.080
and we can start with that education tool.

1537
01:11:40.080 --> 01:11:40.913
I agree.

1538
01:11:40.913 --> 01:11:43.530
Yeah, what else do we want to talk about?

1539
01:11:43.530 --> 01:11:46.710
Let's talk about a day in the life.

1540
01:11:46.710 --> 01:11:48.780
What's the day in the life look for like for you now,

1541
01:11:48.780 --> 01:11:50.670
it's a little bit different than last time we talked.

1542
01:11:50.670 --> 01:11:51.510
It's a little different.

1543
01:11:51.510 --> 01:11:53.070
Complicated.

1544
01:11:53.070 --> 01:11:54.013
Yeah, I wake up--

1545
01:11:54.013 --> 01:11:57.013
I got four, so you know, but they're older now, it's easy.

1546
01:11:57.960 --> 01:11:59.553
Four, it's incredible.

1547
01:12:00.960 --> 01:12:04.149
Day in the life, I used to wake up and meditate,

1548
01:12:04.149 --> 01:12:05.493
now I wake up and feed Athena.

1549
01:12:06.684 --> 01:12:09.330
Well, you gotta wake up in the night too, right?

1550
01:12:09.330 --> 01:12:12.750
I used to, now she sleeps through the night,

1551
01:12:12.750 --> 01:12:14.970
and I don't need to pump during the night,

1552
01:12:14.970 --> 01:12:17.220
but I'll wake up around--

1553
01:12:17.220 --> 01:12:18.090
She sleeps through the night.

1554
01:12:18.090 --> 01:12:20.940
She sleeps through the night, put her down at 6:30,

1555
01:12:20.940 --> 01:12:22.501
she wakes up at 6:30.

1556
01:12:22.501 --> 01:12:23.334
Wow.

1557
01:12:23.334 --> 01:12:24.300
It's pretty remarkable.

1558
01:12:24.300 --> 01:12:25.800
I'm very grateful.

1559
01:12:25.800 --> 01:12:27.630
That's like you won the lottery with that.

1560
01:12:27.630 --> 01:12:29.880
I know I did, I know I won the lottery there.

1561
01:12:29.880 --> 01:12:31.353
So wake up, feed Athena.

1562
01:12:32.490 --> 01:12:34.530
Mornings in our house are really sacred,

1563
01:12:34.530 --> 01:12:37.230
so it's like, we play music,

1564
01:12:37.230 --> 01:12:40.263
it's family time for 90 minutes.

1565
01:12:41.160 --> 01:12:45.393
And then my husband will take the baby, feed her.

1566
01:12:46.500 --> 01:12:50.400
She has a plant-based smoothie in the morning for breakfast.

1567
01:12:50.400 --> 01:12:52.396
Actually, the whole family has the same plant-based meal.

1568
01:12:52.396 --> 01:12:54.148
Yeah, and you're still on the plant-based diet?

1569
01:12:54.148 --> 01:12:55.322
Yeah.

1570
01:12:55.322 --> 01:12:56.396
It's working for you.

1571
01:12:56.396 --> 01:12:57.574
Love it.
Good.

1572
01:12:57.574 --> 01:13:02.130
Like, most of my family members are now plant-based,

1573
01:13:02.130 --> 01:13:05.690
so it's cool to see (laughs).

1574
01:13:05.690 --> 01:13:06.523
That's good.

1575
01:13:06.523 --> 01:13:08.370
It's spread a little bit.

1576
01:13:08.370 --> 01:13:11.820
Did people tell you that you should modify that

1577
01:13:11.820 --> 01:13:15.180
when you were pregnant, and how was the experience

1578
01:13:15.180 --> 01:13:17.310
of being plant-based through pregnancy?

1579
01:13:17.310 --> 01:13:20.760
'Cause I think that's something a lot of plant-curious

1580
01:13:20.760 --> 01:13:24.540
or even plant-based people think about women

1581
01:13:24.540 --> 01:13:26.437
when they get pregnant, like,

1582
01:13:26.437 --> 01:13:30.180
"Hey, maybe I need more iron, or I need more of this."

1583
01:13:30.180 --> 01:13:32.940
And obviously, nobody wants to do anything

1584
01:13:32.940 --> 01:13:34.671
that would be harmful to their baby.

1585
01:13:34.671 --> 01:13:35.629
Yeah, of course.

1586
01:13:35.629 --> 01:13:37.860
Well, I think because I was already a plant-based athlete,

1587
01:13:37.860 --> 01:13:39.420
it answered a lot of those questions.

1588
01:13:39.420 --> 01:13:42.870
So I already had blood work to understand like,

1589
01:13:42.870 --> 01:13:46.233
what are my vitamin D, B, 12, and iron levels?

1590
01:13:47.340 --> 01:13:49.290
And I do supplement all three.

1591
01:13:49.290 --> 01:13:51.690
And the protein question that

1592
01:13:51.690 --> 01:13:55.260
is like the classic plant-based protein question,

1593
01:13:55.260 --> 01:13:57.870
I already had a understanding of how much protein

1594
01:13:57.870 --> 01:14:01.020
I was getting to sustain very, very, two, three hours

1595
01:14:01.020 --> 01:14:02.910
of training a day, most days.

1596
01:14:02.910 --> 01:14:04.800
And so going into pregnancy,

1597
01:14:04.800 --> 01:14:06.810
I kind of knew what my baseline was,

1598
01:14:06.810 --> 01:14:08.430
and that it was actually well above

1599
01:14:08.430 --> 01:14:09.720
and beyond what they recommend

1600
01:14:09.720 --> 01:14:11.190
for a pregnant human,

1601
01:14:11.190 --> 01:14:14.040
let alone a pregnant plant-based athlete.

1602
01:14:14.040 --> 01:14:16.470
So yeah, I felt really confident.

1603
01:14:16.470 --> 01:14:19.920
And to add to it, the fact that it was a type one pregnancy,

1604
01:14:19.920 --> 01:14:21.360
and you wanna keep your blood sugar

1605
01:14:21.360 --> 01:14:23.760
within non-diabetic ranges,

1606
01:14:23.760 --> 01:14:25.620
I wasn't gonna upset the apple cart too much.

1607
01:14:25.620 --> 01:14:27.960
It was like, if I know if I can eat this, this, and that,

1608
01:14:27.960 --> 01:14:31.110
and this is what works before and after training,

1609
01:14:31.110 --> 01:14:32.460
and different parts of my day,

1610
01:14:32.460 --> 01:14:34.410
you know, the nuances that we just discussed

1611
01:14:34.410 --> 01:14:36.856
as someone living with type one.

1612
01:14:36.856 --> 01:14:38.640
I was just gonna keep rocking with that,

1613
01:14:38.640 --> 01:14:40.860
and it worked well for me.

1614
01:14:40.860 --> 01:14:43.547
Good, all right, so plant-based smoothie in the morning.

1615
01:14:43.547 --> 01:14:44.550
Oh, plant-based smoothie in the morning,

1616
01:14:44.550 --> 01:14:46.080
and then I'll do my first training session.

1617
01:14:46.080 --> 01:14:49.290
So usually in the morning, I have a lifting session,

1618
01:14:49.290 --> 01:14:51.510
barbell work, everything from--

1619
01:14:51.510 --> 01:14:52.830
Just at home?

1620
01:14:52.830 --> 01:14:55.560
I'll go to a private training facility to do that.

1621
01:14:55.560 --> 01:15:00.560
And then three or four days a week, I'll do a run.

1622
01:15:01.230 --> 01:15:04.620
So 45 minutes to an hour of running,

1623
01:15:04.620 --> 01:15:09.000
and then I'll normally teach midmorning

1624
01:15:09.000 --> 01:15:11.370
to afternoon, a Peloton.

1625
01:15:11.370 --> 01:15:14.820
So I'll teach a handful of classes four days a week.

1626
01:15:14.820 --> 01:15:17.460
Do you bank those in a row, or how does that work?

1627
01:15:17.460 --> 01:15:21.420
Usually, so a few days a week I teach live classes

1628
01:15:21.420 --> 01:15:23.580
where it'll be like, a 30-minute and a 20-minute.

1629
01:15:23.580 --> 01:15:25.200
Or a 45.

1630
01:15:25.200 --> 01:15:27.550
Usually, it's an under an hour of content live,

1631
01:15:28.470 --> 01:15:31.170
and then sometimes I'm teaching up to two hours

1632
01:15:31.170 --> 01:15:32.460
of content banked.

1633
01:15:32.460 --> 01:15:36.000
So it might be like a bunch of,

1634
01:15:36.000 --> 01:15:37.500
might be as many as five classes,

1635
01:15:37.500 --> 01:15:40.020
but like about two hours of content total.

1636
01:15:40.020 --> 01:15:40.853
Yeah.

1637
01:15:42.142 --> 01:15:42.975
And then...

1638
01:15:42.975 --> 01:15:43.808
Then it's meetings.

1639
01:15:43.808 --> 01:15:47.400
It's either meetings with my agent, with Drew,

1640
01:15:47.400 --> 01:15:50.411
on some of the projects that we're working on.

1641
01:15:50.411 --> 01:15:53.250
Afternoons are usually like, meetings

1642
01:15:53.250 --> 01:15:56.790
or any kind of written, or like mind solitary,

1643
01:15:56.790 --> 01:15:58.690
like, computer work that I have to do.

1644
01:16:00.900 --> 01:16:02.040
Do you have an assistant like--

1645
01:16:02.040 --> 01:16:03.056
I do.

1646
01:16:03.056 --> 01:16:03.889
You do?

1647
01:16:03.889 --> 01:16:05.370
My gosh, yeah, I would not function

1648
01:16:05.370 --> 01:16:09.720
without my assistant, Karen, (laughs) for sure.

1649
01:16:09.720 --> 01:16:11.190
And then early to bed?

1650
01:16:11.190 --> 01:16:12.390
Early to bed, yeah.

1651
01:16:12.390 --> 01:16:15.720
Then it's like, make dinner, wrap up the day,

1652
01:16:15.720 --> 01:16:17.250
hop on, after we put Athena to bed,

1653
01:16:17.250 --> 01:16:18.960
it's usually back on the computer for a little bit,

1654
01:16:18.960 --> 01:16:20.550
for like, more emails and stuff.

1655
01:16:20.550 --> 01:16:23.280
And then I go to bed pretty early, yeah.

1656
01:16:23.280 --> 01:16:25.350
By eight, nine o'clock, it's a wrap.

1657
01:16:25.350 --> 01:16:28.320
Yeah, that's pretty manageable.

1658
01:16:28.320 --> 01:16:32.123
Yes, it really is, I've created a life that aligns

1659
01:16:35.070 --> 01:16:36.540
with how I wanna present in the world,

1660
01:16:36.540 --> 01:16:41.540
and I'm grateful as hell that I get to honestly see

1661
01:16:43.200 --> 01:16:45.060
my daughter as much as I do.

1662
01:16:45.060 --> 01:16:49.680
Like, I remember my mom, who's a practicing physician still.

1663
01:16:49.680 --> 01:16:51.300
So there were some days she would leave at seven

1664
01:16:51.300 --> 01:16:53.220
in the morning and get back at seven at night.

1665
01:16:53.220 --> 01:16:56.550
And at least now, even though I'm busy

1666
01:16:56.550 --> 01:16:58.440
and my daughter is with our nanny who we love

1667
01:16:58.440 --> 01:16:59.880
and I'm very grateful for,

1668
01:16:59.880 --> 01:17:02.180
I can dip in and out and at least see her for 15,

1669
01:17:02.180 --> 01:17:04.230
20 minutes throughout the day,

1670
01:17:04.230 --> 01:17:07.890
and still feel like I'm honoring my work life

1671
01:17:07.890 --> 01:17:08.940
and being productive.

1672
01:17:08.940 --> 01:17:10.500
And how does it work with social media?

1673
01:17:10.500 --> 01:17:11.703
I mean, you have almost like a million people

1674
01:17:11.703 --> 01:17:13.350
that are following you on Instagram.

1675
01:17:13.350 --> 01:17:18.350
Do you feel pressure to be posting and sharing insights,

1676
01:17:18.720 --> 01:17:22.830
or how do you kind of calibrate how you interact

1677
01:17:22.830 --> 01:17:25.020
with that to maintain some level

1678
01:17:25.020 --> 01:17:28.380
of privacy and groundedness?

1679
01:17:28.380 --> 01:17:30.300
Because that's a lot of people.

1680
01:17:30.300 --> 01:17:32.850
I suspect probably you feel some pressure

1681
01:17:32.850 --> 01:17:34.410
to be servicing that.

1682
01:17:34.410 --> 01:17:36.750
Yeah, I mean, the storytelling

1683
01:17:36.750 --> 01:17:38.250
around that is very organic.

1684
01:17:38.250 --> 01:17:40.620
So we kind of have,

1685
01:17:40.620 --> 01:17:42.510
I've developed the social media strategy

1686
01:17:42.510 --> 01:17:43.890
and I kind of know what's gonna live

1687
01:17:43.890 --> 01:17:45.180
in different verticals of that.

1688
01:17:45.180 --> 01:17:48.120
So like, there's like the Peloton stuff, there's motivation,

1689
01:17:48.120 --> 01:17:50.070
there's the mom stuff, you know?

1690
01:17:50.070 --> 01:17:52.350
And then there's stuff like this week

1691
01:17:52.350 --> 01:17:55.773
where I'm just posting organically when I feel like it.

1692
01:17:59.880 --> 01:18:01.620
I had to decide a long time ago

1693
01:18:01.620 --> 01:18:03.570
to take the pressure off of that.

1694
01:18:03.570 --> 01:18:04.430
So it's...

1695
01:18:05.512 --> 01:18:06.480
You don't have rules?

1696
01:18:06.480 --> 01:18:08.495
Like, every day I do this or absolutely not.

1697
01:18:08.495 --> 01:18:09.328
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1698
01:18:09.328 --> 01:18:10.161
Absolutely not, no.

1699
01:18:10.161 --> 01:18:11.430
That's the only way that I can stay sane

1700
01:18:11.430 --> 01:18:12.630
with the whole thing.

1701
01:18:12.630 --> 01:18:14.670
Like, my rule is like, I only post when I feel like it,

1702
01:18:14.670 --> 01:18:16.923
and if I don't feel like it, like,

1703
01:18:17.992 --> 01:18:20.070
you just can't be held hostage by it.

1704
01:18:20.070 --> 01:18:21.872
Like, it's just not healthy.

1705
01:18:21.872 --> 01:18:23.550
I agree, do you have notifications on your phone

1706
01:18:23.550 --> 01:18:24.482
from all these things?

1707
01:18:24.482 --> 01:18:27.360
No, I don't have any notifications for anything.

1708
01:18:27.360 --> 01:18:28.457
Me neither (laughs).

1709
01:18:29.370 --> 01:18:31.200
So then like in the household though,

1710
01:18:31.200 --> 01:18:33.090
like, one of my kids is texting me,

1711
01:18:33.090 --> 01:18:35.340
and I don't get, its like, if I'm not checking my phone,

1712
01:18:35.340 --> 01:18:37.620
they're like, "I texted you (indistinct)."

1713
01:18:37.620 --> 01:18:38.453
Sorry--

1714
01:18:38.453 --> 01:18:42.210
Well, this perception of imediacy

1715
01:18:42.210 --> 01:18:46.440
of in response to anything,

1716
01:18:46.440 --> 01:18:48.690
I mean, I remember, when you practice law,

1717
01:18:48.690 --> 01:18:50.580
people probably still sent written letters.

1718
01:18:50.580 --> 01:18:51.653
Yeah, of course.

1719
01:18:51.653 --> 01:18:52.927
(laughs) So there was like, a list--

1720
01:18:52.927 --> 01:18:54.900
Which was in art form,

1721
01:18:54.900 --> 01:18:56.370
especially like the demand letter.

1722
01:18:56.370 --> 01:18:58.710
Like, "How snarky can I be in this letter?"

1723
01:18:58.710 --> 01:19:01.380
You pour over it for hours.

1724
01:19:01.380 --> 01:19:06.380
Yeah, I mean, and I'm prey to, just like anybody else,

1725
01:19:06.810 --> 01:19:11.693
you know, the distractions and this perception

1726
01:19:14.310 --> 01:19:19.310
that time is, that we need to be as immediate

1727
01:19:19.470 --> 01:19:22.920
with our response as it was in the sender's perception,

1728
01:19:22.920 --> 01:19:27.222
and I really try to release myself from those expectations.

1729
01:19:27.222 --> 01:19:30.810
Yeah, that's healthy, and go to Coachella.

1730
01:19:30.810 --> 01:19:31.767
Exactly (laughs).

1731
01:19:34.290 --> 01:19:35.961
What else do I want to talk to you about?

1732
01:19:35.961 --> 01:19:40.530
I mean, I think it's really cool what you've built,

1733
01:19:40.530 --> 01:19:42.780
and what you stand for, and what you've done,

1734
01:19:42.780 --> 01:19:44.370
and to like own your strength,

1735
01:19:44.370 --> 01:19:49.110
I think is really not just powerful, but infectious.

1736
01:19:49.110 --> 01:19:50.940
And I think part of your appeal,

1737
01:19:50.940 --> 01:19:53.730
if I had to guess, is that everyone wants

1738
01:19:53.730 --> 01:19:55.680
to feel a little bit more empowered.

1739
01:19:55.680 --> 01:19:58.530
Like, they see you talking about crowns

1740
01:19:58.530 --> 01:20:00.867
and owning your space and all of that.

1741
01:20:00.867 --> 01:20:04.440
And I think to some degree, we all self-coagulate

1742
01:20:04.440 --> 01:20:06.720
and we all have insecurities.

1743
01:20:06.720 --> 01:20:10.027
And to see you as this (indistinct) going,

1744
01:20:10.027 --> 01:20:11.880
"No, do this instead,"

1745
01:20:11.880 --> 01:20:14.050
like, I need a little bit more of that in my life.

1746
01:20:14.050 --> 01:20:16.110
Like, I want that.

1747
01:20:16.110 --> 01:20:19.170
So for people that are feeling that urge,

1748
01:20:19.170 --> 01:20:20.910
or that pull, or that yearning

1749
01:20:20.910 --> 01:20:23.572
for a little bit more self-efficacy

1750
01:20:23.572 --> 01:20:26.820
and agency in their life,

1751
01:20:26.820 --> 01:20:28.680
maybe it would be helpful to just kind

1752
01:20:28.680 --> 01:20:32.610
of share spontaneously a little bit of that wisdom.

1753
01:20:32.610 --> 01:20:35.850
Sure, I mean, I do think a lot

1754
01:20:35.850 --> 01:20:40.850
of your listeners have some type of movement practice.

1755
01:20:41.250 --> 01:20:46.250
I think that movement has changed my life.

1756
01:20:46.680 --> 01:20:50.700
I didn't know I wasn't an athlete as a kid.

1757
01:20:50.700 --> 01:20:53.340
I was completely terrified of gym class.

1758
01:20:53.340 --> 01:20:56.310
I told myself I was like, I used to get made fun of

1759
01:20:56.310 --> 01:20:59.220
for the way I ran when I was in recess or whatever.

1760
01:20:59.220 --> 01:21:01.440
So I told myself this story at a really young age

1761
01:21:01.440 --> 01:21:04.170
that I was the straight A student arts and crafts kid.

1762
01:21:04.170 --> 01:21:06.600
And that's who I was.

1763
01:21:06.600 --> 01:21:09.960
And I am introverted by nature,

1764
01:21:09.960 --> 01:21:13.200
so those, being good at school and doing arts

1765
01:21:13.200 --> 01:21:15.630
and crafts really suited my nature.

1766
01:21:15.630 --> 01:21:20.630
And movement has unlocked a wellspring of confidence.

1767
01:21:23.700 --> 01:21:28.590
And I do believe that confidence is a side effect of hustle.

1768
01:21:28.590 --> 01:21:33.590
I think that you have to really drill down on what form

1769
01:21:34.170 --> 01:21:37.860
of hustle feels rewarding, and is getting you closer

1770
01:21:37.860 --> 01:21:40.920
to the finish line of your own definition.

1771
01:21:40.920 --> 01:21:45.120
And a regular movement practice is crucial,

1772
01:21:45.120 --> 01:21:46.670
no matter what that looks like.

1773
01:21:48.210 --> 01:21:53.210
For me, another tool in the toolkit is storytelling.

1774
01:21:56.640 --> 01:22:00.213
Like, I think when we're brave enough to tell our stories,

1775
01:22:11.640 --> 01:22:13.710
it frees up our ability to imagine our,

1776
01:22:13.710 --> 01:22:15.390
the answers to those questions, right?

1777
01:22:15.390 --> 01:22:16.680
It's like, what is my why?

1778
01:22:16.680 --> 01:22:19.500
What decision would I make if I were twice as confident?

1779
01:22:19.500 --> 01:22:21.960
Those existential, who am I moments.

1780
01:22:21.960 --> 01:22:23.700
Why not me?

1781
01:22:23.700 --> 01:22:27.060
Those things are always kind of percolating in my head,

1782
01:22:27.060 --> 01:22:31.270
but it's really crucial to me that folks

1783
01:22:33.330 --> 01:22:36.300
give themselves permission to win.

1784
01:22:36.300 --> 01:22:38.700
Where sometimes success feels as uncomfortable

1785
01:22:38.700 --> 01:22:41.040
as the work that it took to get there,

1786
01:22:41.040 --> 01:22:46.040
and I want folks to give themselves permission

1787
01:22:47.820 --> 01:22:49.200
to step into the light.

1788
01:22:49.200 --> 01:22:52.050
Like, I wanna stand closer to the folks

1789
01:22:52.050 --> 01:22:56.430
who illuminate you, and even pay attention

1790
01:22:56.430 --> 01:22:59.130
to who and what that is.

1791
01:22:59.130 --> 01:23:02.610
Like, most people would have to think long

1792
01:23:02.610 --> 01:23:05.760
and hard about the experiences that even bring them

1793
01:23:05.760 --> 01:23:07.650
the most joy are the people that they're with

1794
01:23:07.650 --> 01:23:08.730
when they experience joy

1795
01:23:08.730 --> 01:23:11.673
or what joy even feels like recently.

1796
01:23:13.830 --> 01:23:16.080
We should be centering those conversations more.

1797
01:23:16.080 --> 01:23:17.520
Yeah, I think that's powerful.

1798
01:23:17.520 --> 01:23:20.550
That goes to that second tier that we were talking

1799
01:23:20.550 --> 01:23:22.500
about, the who am I thing.

1800
01:23:22.500 --> 01:23:25.110
And I think it is true that most people

1801
01:23:25.110 --> 01:23:28.770
are so sufficiently disconnected that they aren't aware

1802
01:23:28.770 --> 01:23:30.000
of what brings them joy,

1803
01:23:30.000 --> 01:23:31.920
or what is driving their decisions,

1804
01:23:31.920 --> 01:23:34.950
or the activities that would guard their life

1805
01:23:34.950 --> 01:23:37.770
with more purpose and fulfillment and meaning and the like.

1806
01:23:37.770 --> 01:23:40.290
And I think a process of starting

1807
01:23:40.290 --> 01:23:42.090
to better understand yourself

1808
01:23:42.090 --> 01:23:45.210
and what makes you tick is yes in the journaling,

1809
01:23:45.210 --> 01:23:47.373
but also, as you mentioned,

1810
01:23:48.390 --> 01:23:49.980
having the courage to share your story,

1811
01:23:49.980 --> 01:23:52.110
we all have a story, right?

1812
01:23:52.110 --> 01:23:56.100
And that courage is born out of a choice to be vulnerable.

1813
01:23:56.100 --> 01:23:59.700
Like, the vulnerability is strength because it takes bravery

1814
01:23:59.700 --> 01:24:02.550
and courage to share something and allow yourself

1815
01:24:02.550 --> 01:24:04.890
to be vulnerable and judged for that.

1816
01:24:04.890 --> 01:24:07.620
But my experience and the experience

1817
01:24:07.620 --> 01:24:09.750
of many others that I've born witness to,

1818
01:24:09.750 --> 01:24:13.110
and yourself as well, is that when you can summon that

1819
01:24:13.110 --> 01:24:16.110
and engage with that, like, grapple with that,

1820
01:24:16.110 --> 01:24:19.110
there is a level of self-efficacy and self-esteem

1821
01:24:19.110 --> 01:24:20.910
that derives from that that allows you

1822
01:24:20.910 --> 01:24:24.570
to be more self-integrated and self-actualized

1823
01:24:24.570 --> 01:24:27.600
to stand in that power, to adorn that crown.

1824
01:24:27.600 --> 01:24:30.630
And like, claim your truth and your path.

1825
01:24:30.630 --> 01:24:32.370
Well, speaking your story, speaking your truth

1826
01:24:32.370 --> 01:24:34.023
is gonna magnetize your people.

1827
01:24:35.160 --> 01:24:39.210
I think about, that's what I've done,

1828
01:24:39.210 --> 01:24:41.790
of course, it's on a very public platform,

1829
01:24:41.790 --> 01:24:43.620
be it social media or Peloton,

1830
01:24:43.620 --> 01:24:46.380
but speaking my truth has magnetized people

1831
01:24:46.380 --> 01:24:48.870
who that truth resonates with.

1832
01:24:48.870 --> 01:24:51.000
Yeah, that's the lighthouse thing.

1833
01:24:51.000 --> 01:24:52.170
That's the light, exactly,

1834
01:24:52.170 --> 01:24:53.003
it's the lighthouse thing, is like,

1835
01:24:53.003 --> 01:24:57.420
how are your people gonna find you if you're confused,

1836
01:24:57.420 --> 01:25:00.663
misaligned, and obfuscating who you are?

1837
01:25:02.370 --> 01:25:05.400
And then part of movement

1838
01:25:05.400 --> 01:25:08.370
and our movement practices are an ability

1839
01:25:08.370 --> 01:25:10.503
to discover who that person even is.

1840
01:25:11.760 --> 01:25:13.110
Something that I ask myself

1841
01:25:14.010 --> 01:25:16.680
as a touch point pretty frequently are like,

1842
01:25:16.680 --> 01:25:19.050
what are my recent moments of flow?

1843
01:25:19.050 --> 01:25:21.600
Am I still finding moments of flow

1844
01:25:21.600 --> 01:25:22.980
where I'm losing track of time?

1845
01:25:22.980 --> 01:25:24.600
Not because I'm scrolling on social media,

1846
01:25:24.600 --> 01:25:28.830
but because I'm so immersed with focus and alignment,

1847
01:25:28.830 --> 01:25:31.653
feelings of alignment and fulfillment.

1848
01:25:33.456 --> 01:25:36.992
Am I centering those experiences throughout my work day?

1849
01:25:36.992 --> 01:25:38.010
And I definitely go through periods

1850
01:25:38.010 --> 01:25:40.230
where that isn't as much the case.

1851
01:25:40.230 --> 01:25:41.193
And it's like my what.

1852
01:25:41.193 --> 01:25:42.026
It's more disciplined

1853
01:25:42.026 --> 01:25:42.859
to do that now--
So much.

1854
01:25:42.859 --> 01:25:44.220
Than it ever has before.

1855
01:25:44.220 --> 01:25:46.350
But I would say most people don't ask themselves

1856
01:25:46.350 --> 01:25:51.267
that question of, "When am I experiencing moments of flow?"

1857
01:25:53.070 --> 01:25:57.990
Yeah, it's so easy and reflexive to just grab the phone.

1858
01:25:57.990 --> 01:26:01.950
My eldest stepson, Tyler, just got the light phone,

1859
01:26:01.950 --> 01:26:05.490
which has nothing on it except only like, texting.

1860
01:26:05.490 --> 01:26:08.340
And he's like, "In the first week, I read two books."

1861
01:26:08.340 --> 01:26:09.173
You know, and he's like,

1862
01:26:09.173 --> 01:26:12.150
"I'm doing all these things that I'd never done before."

1863
01:26:12.150 --> 01:26:15.030
And he has his other iPhone, he just has it in a drawer,

1864
01:26:15.030 --> 01:26:16.920
you know, and checks it once in a while or whatever.

1865
01:26:16.920 --> 01:26:18.883
But when he is out in the world, he's like, "Yeah,

1866
01:26:18.883 --> 01:26:21.390
I was standing in a line the other day and there was nothing

1867
01:26:21.390 --> 01:26:23.700
for me to do except be with myself."

1868
01:26:23.700 --> 01:26:25.323
And it was like a revelation.

1869
01:26:26.490 --> 01:26:30.750
And I'm as guilty as anyone of that to exercise,

1870
01:26:30.750 --> 01:26:33.690
as a disciplined person, I find myself powerless at times

1871
01:26:33.690 --> 01:26:37.110
to exercise the discipline required to carve out

1872
01:26:37.110 --> 01:26:39.420
the quiet time to do the deep work,

1873
01:26:39.420 --> 01:26:41.493
to accomplish the more important things just.

1874
01:26:41.493 --> 01:26:43.320
Just to even be bored.

1875
01:26:43.320 --> 01:26:46.540
I actually, on that note, I made a rule this year

1876
01:26:48.240 --> 01:26:51.603
to not grab my phone when I'm in elevators.

1877
01:26:52.680 --> 01:26:55.740
And even when I'm like,

1878
01:26:55.740 --> 01:26:57.570
just on the cusp of sending an email,

1879
01:26:57.570 --> 01:27:01.260
when I get into the elevator, to force myself to like,

1880
01:27:01.260 --> 01:27:03.663
even just be aware and have a human interaction.

1881
01:27:04.950 --> 01:27:07.020
But then you're like, it's creepy.

1882
01:27:07.020 --> 01:27:09.354
Now, I force myself in that.

1883
01:27:09.354 --> 01:27:14.354
And I am very allergic to like, (laughs)

1884
01:27:14.720 --> 01:27:16.290
I don't like small talk,

1885
01:27:16.290 --> 01:27:20.100
but I find that it's like, just let your mind...

1886
01:27:20.100 --> 01:27:23.220
When's the last time that I let my mind wander?

1887
01:27:23.220 --> 01:27:25.770
I'm always just, again, grabbing from my phone,

1888
01:27:25.770 --> 01:27:27.837
refreshing the email, like, checking Instagram.

1889
01:27:27.837 --> 01:27:30.570
And the wandering mind is the bread and butter

1890
01:27:30.570 --> 01:27:32.717
of any creative inspiration.

1891
01:27:32.717 --> 01:27:34.863
And if you deprive yourself of that,

1892
01:27:35.790 --> 01:27:37.713
it's a form of self-harm.

1893
01:27:40.380 --> 01:27:42.390
Journaling also helps, 'cause there are some days

1894
01:27:42.390 --> 01:27:44.100
where I don't feel like I have anything to journal,

1895
01:27:44.100 --> 01:27:46.890
but I'll just set five minutes, a five-minute time

1896
01:27:46.890 --> 01:27:49.200
around my phone and something always pours out.

1897
01:27:49.200 --> 01:27:51.030
So what is the structure to the journaling?

1898
01:27:51.030 --> 01:27:53.220
Is it like morning pages where it's just sort

1899
01:27:53.220 --> 01:27:54.720
of a vomitous thing?

1900
01:27:54.720 --> 01:27:58.170
Or do you approach it with a certain intention?

1901
01:27:58.170 --> 01:28:01.200
Usually, it's in the evening actually, before I go to bed,

1902
01:28:01.200 --> 01:28:05.943
I find that my mornings are a lot focused on Athena.

1903
01:28:06.870 --> 01:28:08.880
So it's evening focused.

1904
01:28:08.880 --> 01:28:12.300
And sometimes there's an emotionality

1905
01:28:12.300 --> 01:28:14.040
to it where it's just like,

1906
01:28:14.040 --> 01:28:15.420
I'm frustrated about this or that,

1907
01:28:15.420 --> 01:28:18.630
and it's more of like a dear diary moment.

1908
01:28:18.630 --> 01:28:21.330
But the days where I don't feel like I have anything

1909
01:28:21.330 --> 01:28:25.530
to write, I will literally just set a timer and dream.

1910
01:28:25.530 --> 01:28:29.250
You know, whether it's like, I'll ask myself questions,

1911
01:28:29.250 --> 01:28:32.190
like, who is a dream partnership?

1912
01:28:32.190 --> 01:28:35.010
Or like, what would success look like in Q1?

1913
01:28:35.010 --> 01:28:36.990
You know, how are you defining that for yourself?

1914
01:28:36.990 --> 01:28:39.120
So sometimes it's like, business ideations,

1915
01:28:39.120 --> 01:28:41.490
and sometimes it's more personal.

1916
01:28:41.490 --> 01:28:42.780
And then it's that gratitude thing.

1917
01:28:42.780 --> 01:28:45.187
I ask myself that pretty frequently, like,

1918
01:28:45.187 --> 01:28:47.250
"What do you have right now that you used to dream about

1919
01:28:47.250 --> 01:28:48.360
or pray for?"

1920
01:28:48.360 --> 01:28:52.470
And I also find that with a gratitude practice,

1921
01:28:52.470 --> 01:28:56.400
there's a recency that helps saying three things

1922
01:28:56.400 --> 01:28:57.233
that you're grateful for.

1923
01:28:57.233 --> 01:29:00.210
Like, I'm always gonna say Athena, Drew, my family.

1924
01:29:00.210 --> 01:29:03.120
And then that kind of takes away from the special feeling.

1925
01:29:03.120 --> 01:29:04.950
Being very specific and temporal.

1926
01:29:04.950 --> 01:29:07.950
Yeah, temporal, like, it'll be like, in the last 12 hours,

1927
01:29:07.950 --> 01:29:10.530
what's something that you're grateful for that occurred,

1928
01:29:10.530 --> 01:29:14.340
or that you witnessed in that recent time period?

1929
01:29:14.340 --> 01:29:16.080
And that helps too.

1930
01:29:16.080 --> 01:29:17.640
And what goes into the morning smoothie?

1931
01:29:17.640 --> 01:29:21.690
Oh gosh, my husband is actually the smoothie king.

1932
01:29:21.690 --> 01:29:22.980
So I use AG1.

1933
01:29:22.980 --> 01:29:23.913
He's jacked.

1934
01:29:23.913 --> 01:29:24.746
He is jacked (laughs).

1935
01:29:24.746 --> 01:29:25.860
He's plant-based too?

1936
01:29:25.860 --> 01:29:26.962
Plant based.

1937
01:29:26.962 --> 01:29:28.730
He's completely ripped.

1938
01:29:28.730 --> 01:29:30.750
He's a strong individual (laughs).

1939
01:29:30.750 --> 01:29:32.810
He's very strong young man.

1940
01:29:32.810 --> 01:29:35.100
So yeah, we use AG1 plant protein,

1941
01:29:35.100 --> 01:29:37.650
we mix up what kind of protein we use all the time.

1942
01:29:38.550 --> 01:29:43.500
Magnesium, vitamin D, greens like spinach, kale,

1943
01:29:43.500 --> 01:29:45.150
whatever we have in the fridge.

1944
01:29:45.150 --> 01:29:48.420
Yeah, there's like, 17 ingredients (laughs).

1945
01:29:48.420 --> 01:29:50.190
And how has your marriage evolved

1946
01:29:50.190 --> 01:29:52.890
from being just the two of you to being three?

1947
01:29:52.890 --> 01:29:54.900
Like, what are the things that you've learned

1948
01:29:54.900 --> 01:29:56.280
about being in partnership?

1949
01:29:56.280 --> 01:29:58.380
And also now, like this Web 3 thing,

1950
01:29:58.380 --> 01:30:01.410
like, you're kind of, commingled a little bit professionally

1951
01:30:01.410 --> 01:30:03.420
in a way that you weren't previously.

1952
01:30:03.420 --> 01:30:05.040
What are the challenges that you faced,

1953
01:30:05.040 --> 01:30:07.080
and what are some of the lessons that you learned

1954
01:30:07.080 --> 01:30:11.010
about what's required to stay together?

1955
01:30:11.010 --> 01:30:14.050
Wasn't until we had Athena that I understood

1956
01:30:16.710 --> 01:30:20.070
that it was like, I have my life, Drew has his life,

1957
01:30:20.070 --> 01:30:22.200
and then our marriage is like, another life

1958
01:30:22.200 --> 01:30:23.580
that we both pour into.

1959
01:30:23.580 --> 01:30:26.370
Whereas it all felt very like, yeah, that's my husband,

1960
01:30:26.370 --> 01:30:28.530
of course, I love him, we do our thing.

1961
01:30:28.530 --> 01:30:32.490
Now it's the date nights and making sure that we have time

1962
01:30:32.490 --> 01:30:35.940
for each other, which all just, it was like, every,

1963
01:30:35.940 --> 01:30:38.700
all the time was time with each other before Athena,

1964
01:30:38.700 --> 01:30:43.700
and now as life busies, that has now risen to the top

1965
01:30:46.170 --> 01:30:47.430
of the list of making sure

1966
01:30:47.430 --> 01:30:49.890
that I'm prioritizing my time with him.

1967
01:30:49.890 --> 01:30:52.350
And we've done a great job, travel

1968
01:30:52.350 --> 01:30:55.770
and adventure is part of who is in the DNA

1969
01:30:55.770 --> 01:30:57.060
of our relationship.

1970
01:30:57.060 --> 01:31:01.680
We actually got engaged 72 hours after meeting each other,

1971
01:31:01.680 --> 01:31:04.423
and then got married at Burning Man three months later.

1972
01:31:04.423 --> 01:31:05.256
I know that, I knew that.

1973
01:31:05.256 --> 01:31:09.290
So we believe we're like multiple lifetimes in,

1974
01:31:09.290 --> 01:31:10.850
and we have a very cosmic connection.

1975
01:31:10.850 --> 01:31:13.380
(indistinct) in marriage, but then you got married

1976
01:31:13.380 --> 01:31:14.213
into loom, right?

1977
01:31:14.213 --> 01:31:17.310
Yeah, so we had a Burning Man ceremony three months

1978
01:31:17.310 --> 01:31:18.390
after meeting each other,

1979
01:31:18.390 --> 01:31:21.000
and then we had a public ceremony

1980
01:31:21.000 --> 01:31:22.503
into loom a few years later.

1981
01:31:23.370 --> 01:31:26.460
But making sure that we're planning trips,

1982
01:31:26.460 --> 01:31:27.600
whether it's this,

1983
01:31:27.600 --> 01:31:29.190
we're going to a friend's wedding in Ireland,

1984
01:31:29.190 --> 01:31:31.080
we're going back to Burning Man.

1985
01:31:31.080 --> 01:31:34.320
And of course, we have trips planned with Athena as well.

1986
01:31:34.320 --> 01:31:36.780
We're making sure that we're adventuring as a family,

1987
01:31:36.780 --> 01:31:38.640
but also as a couple and individually,

1988
01:31:38.640 --> 01:31:42.870
we've both with each taken trips alone since having Athena.

1989
01:31:42.870 --> 01:31:46.053
And we're kind of trying to find the right balance,

1990
01:31:46.920 --> 01:31:49.740
intentional balance and alignment with our values

1991
01:31:49.740 --> 01:31:52.620
of individual time, and then as a family,

1992
01:31:52.620 --> 01:31:53.550
and then as a couple.

1993
01:31:53.550 --> 01:31:54.990
Anything unforeseen,

1994
01:31:54.990 --> 01:31:57.003
or a challenge that you didn't expect?

1995
01:32:02.370 --> 01:32:06.000
We communicate like, I'm very clear

1996
01:32:06.000 --> 01:32:08.730
with what I need, whether, I think--

1997
01:32:08.730 --> 01:32:10.140
That's important.

1998
01:32:10.140 --> 01:32:12.000
I think that helps a lot.

1999
01:32:12.000 --> 01:32:14.170
I don't expect him to be a mind reader and likewise.

2000
01:32:14.170 --> 01:32:17.520
Yeah, 'cause for a lot of partnerships, it's sort of like,

2001
01:32:17.520 --> 01:32:20.760
you have to do a little bit of mind reading or anticipation

2002
01:32:20.760 --> 01:32:22.500
and the more clear that you can be.

2003
01:32:22.500 --> 01:32:24.690
I think everybody benefits from that,

2004
01:32:24.690 --> 01:32:26.020
even if it's an uncomfortable truth.

2005
01:32:26.020 --> 01:32:27.900
Yeah, yeah, I'm pretty clear.

2006
01:32:27.900 --> 01:32:31.740
I mean, (laughs) I remember, Drew was really,

2007
01:32:31.740 --> 01:32:34.890
it was incredibly an incredible partner,

2008
01:32:34.890 --> 01:32:37.440
you know, when Athena was first home,

2009
01:32:37.440 --> 01:32:38.760
I mean, he's an incredible partner always,

2010
01:32:38.760 --> 01:32:43.740
but especially as it involved caring for a small human,

2011
01:32:43.740 --> 01:32:44.940
our new roommate.

2012
01:32:44.940 --> 01:32:47.730
And I would wake up at two in the morning

2013
01:32:47.730 --> 01:32:49.500
or however many times at night to feed,

2014
01:32:49.500 --> 01:32:51.480
and we set up like, a breastfeeding station

2015
01:32:51.480 --> 01:32:52.860
with like, snacks and whatever.

2016
01:32:52.860 --> 01:32:56.370
And one time he ate the almond butter sandwich

2017
01:32:56.370 --> 01:32:58.680
that I had waiting for, he had prepared,

2018
01:32:58.680 --> 01:33:00.570
but he ate like half of it (laughs).

2019
01:33:00.570 --> 01:33:02.280
It's like a very clear boundary,

2020
01:33:02.280 --> 01:33:06.300
like, you cannot eat my snacks when I'm breastfeeding

2021
01:33:06.300 --> 01:33:07.170
at two in the morning.

2022
01:33:07.170 --> 01:33:09.330
So little things like that are actually big things.

2023
01:33:09.330 --> 01:33:12.420
It's like, we're trying to set each other up for success,

2024
01:33:12.420 --> 01:33:15.300
so no one person needs to feel like a mind reader.

2025
01:33:15.300 --> 01:33:19.603
Yeah, well, the complexity of the child really ups

2026
01:33:21.630 --> 01:33:24.780
the exigency of that communication,

2027
01:33:24.780 --> 01:33:26.610
and the planning and all of that,

2028
01:33:26.610 --> 01:33:30.210
because it's so easy to default to a routine,

2029
01:33:30.210 --> 01:33:32.490
and before you know it, you're leading separate lives

2030
01:33:32.490 --> 01:33:34.050
and you haven't really talked.

2031
01:33:34.050 --> 01:33:37.260
I see it all the time, and it really requires a level

2032
01:33:37.260 --> 01:33:39.367
of conscious awareness to say,

2033
01:33:39.367 --> 01:33:41.700
"Hey, oh, we need to stop.

2034
01:33:41.700 --> 01:33:44.340
We gotta get in sync here, because otherwise,

2035
01:33:44.340 --> 01:33:48.300
we're like, extrapolate that little tiny diversion

2036
01:33:48.300 --> 01:33:51.120
and suddenly, we're in very different places

2037
01:33:51.120 --> 01:33:52.380
in not too long."

2038
01:33:52.380 --> 01:33:56.430
Yeah, well, I'm trying to be aware of the marriage

2039
01:33:56.430 --> 01:33:59.610
as an organism, as well as Drew

2040
01:33:59.610 --> 01:34:03.600
as a sentient being, and myself.

2041
01:34:03.600 --> 01:34:08.310
So it's actually three entities or humans kind

2042
01:34:08.310 --> 01:34:10.460
of that we're curating and we're nurturing.

2043
01:34:11.370 --> 01:34:12.960
And I mean, three as in the marriage,

2044
01:34:12.960 --> 01:34:14.910
and then the two of us individually.

2045
01:34:14.910 --> 01:34:17.160
And then of course, our family is an organism,

2046
01:34:17.160 --> 01:34:18.840
but that's the more obvious one.

2047
01:34:18.840 --> 01:34:22.110
Yeah, I like the idea of the separate trips thing.

